[size=3]1.9.0[/size]

[b]Corvettes.[/b] A new page shows the corvette in your save, the whole ship drawn from the
modules bolted to it, with every module listed by its in-game name and the position it stands at.
Modules sitting at an angle the build menu has no rotation for are marked, since those are the
ones you could not have placed from inside the game.

[b]A builder, in a window of its own.[/b] Pick from a palette of 172 modules, drag them into
place, and save the result back. Drag a module to slide it flat, hold Shift to raise and lower
it, or take hold of the red, green and blue arrows to run along one axis at a time. Rings turn it
about any axis and three sliders do the same by the degree. Undo and redo go back eighty steps.
Snap can be set to the build grid, to a finer step, or off altogether. Mirror a module across the
centreline and the pair moves, turns and goes together.

[b]Parts snap together the way the game does.[/b] Every module carries named sockets and the game
joins two modules by laying one part's socket exactly onto another's. The builder reads those same
sockets, so a part dropped near the ship seats itself and says which socket caught it. Measured
against a ship built in game, fifteen of sixteen modules snap back to the exact position the game
had them in.

[b]Place anything anywhere.[/b] The build menu's restrictions are checked while building and never
re-checked when a save is loaded, so a module written through the builder can stand anywhere, at
any angle, bolted to nothing. Confirmed in game: a turret rolled onto its side, a hab tumbled on
all three axes and a thruster at 37 degrees all load and draw, and the game writes them back
unchanged when it next saves.

[b]Ships render properly where they did not.[/b] Any mesh with more than 65,536 vertices was being
read with the wrong index width, which turned it into twice as many triangles with every one
spanning the model -- corvette turrets and landing gear looked crumpled, and 74 spacecraft meshes
across the game had the same fault. Shield generators are solid again too; the filter that drops a
ship's shield bubble was matching on the word alone and throwing away the corvette's
shield-generator parts with it.

[b]Interceptors show the colour they are actually born in.[/b] The body colour comes from the
overlay the seed draws, not from its palette, so the app could find you a black interceptor and
then draw it orange.

[b]The corvette hull no longer has a ghost inside it.[/b] The scene named after the hull holds
nothing but the build station's holographic preview, which was being drawn inside every corvette
as a blue ship-shaped shell.

[b]Every module in the build menu draws.[/b] Module geometry now comes from the game's parts table
keyed by each module's style rather than from guessing at names, taking coverage from 128 of 194
to 171. The rest are interior walls, whose model depends on the room they sit in.
